{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"I’ll read the findings chain and architecture first, then measure this host and implement the bounded verification and publication in the existing worktree. I’ll preserve the frozen files and leave all changes uncommitted."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md; cat collab/yang-clojure/1794671000001-d1-bounds-declaration-draft.md; cat collab/yang-clojure/1794670000001-*partial.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md; cat collab/yang-clojure/1794671000001-d1-bounds-declaration-draft.md; cat collab/yang-clojure/1794670000001-*partial.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n# D1 bounds declaration — DRAFT (static per-fixture gates; prepares the r9 tagged run)\n\nCreated: 2026-11-14. Role: VM Engineer (compiler provenance), thread\n1794422563000 (D1 correspondence), round r8 deliverable 3.\n\n**Status: DRAFT ONLY — no run has been performed under these ceilings,\nand nothing here authorizes a tagged run or reliance.** This declaration\nis written AFTER the r8 static corpus run (its numbers are that run's\nmeasurements) and BEFORE any r9 tagged verification run, per the partial\nround's requirement that a finite D1 declaration precede any tagged\nrun. The r8 run itself was NOT a ceiling-governed run: it is the\nmeasurement source. The ceilings in section 3 become binding for the\nfirst run declared under them (r9's tagged run), which must re-state\nor tighten them from its own measurements.\n\nThe r8 gates are STATIC: no C lanes were run, no guest fuel consumed, no\ntraces recorded. Lane-quantity ceilings (guest instructions, trace\nrecords and capacity) are therefore NOT declared here; r9 must measure\nand declare them before its run. cc3 v2.1 historical maxima remain\nbaselines only, not D1 numbers.\n\n## 1. Pinned corpus, revision, host, and settings\n\n| Item | Pinned value |\n|---|---|\n| Worktree / branch | /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-d1, branch d1-correspondence @ 56cc5ca3 + the uncommitted r1-r8 diff |\n| Tracked diff (git diff, all of it test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj) | 33,521 B, SHA-256 55c766cb5eef958f26b3ed03cb9169022cb66869fb8734777477fd65cd89dff1 |\n| New untracked code | src/cljc/yang/correspondence_batch.clj; test yang/correspondence_batch_test.clj (all other r1-r7 files unchanged this round) |\n| Corpus (the whole workload) | test/yang/latte_oracle/kernel_gate.edn, SHA-256 8763e273e08259686477315f41956d7005981327b7965bf01e83f9cce487c3e3, 155 fixtures |\n| Frozen v1 evidence | test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_site_tables.edn, SHA-256 e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75 (unchanged since r1) |\n| Run record | collab/yang-clojure/1794422563000-engineer-d1-correspondence-r8.outcomes.edn, SHA-256 7c2f9a6a17bdd144ba67be0326dc4b6ebdb6a83a7297ab08ac82e4ef8aef943a — 155 :pass, 0 :refused, 0 :not-run, with per-fixture admission origin, storage delta, elapsed, row counts, site count, mutation-refusal stage |\n| Upstream oracle | latte-kernel 86fca0d1d054af005cb37395eae04500d45e6279 (deps.edn :test pin) |\n| Host | macOS 26.6.2 arm64, MacBookPro18,3, 17,179,869,184 B RAM |\n| JVM / Clojure | OpenJDK 17.0.18 (Homebrew, 64-Bit Server VM), Clojure 1.12.4, -Xss64m via the :test alias |\n| Driver | d1-corpus-batch! + test-d1-corpus-static-gates in test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj; per-invocation run config target/d1-corpus-run.edn |\n\n## 2. Workload and retained object set\n\nOne fixture static gate, held one fixture at a time (maximum\nsimultaneously retained fixtures: 1; each holder released before the\nnext):\n\n- the admission: a fresh recorded admission coordinate\n  [:source :program [run-scope ordinal] 0] (distinct ordinal per fixture\n  within a batch run scope; fresh scope per batch invocation);\n- the compiled input: compile-program output, stamped AST, site table,\n  reconstruction input (captured forms/call/program/options);\n- the binding: producer C1 tree + rows, stamped tree + rows,\n  correspondence map, site-to-occurrence bindings, and the independent\n  reconstructions checked against them;\n- the persisted set: the whole v1 evidence map (content-addressed,\n  written once for the corpus), the profile (once), and per fixture the\n  reconstruction input, the binding snapshot, and BOTH row closures as\n  individually addressed rows (the 128-nesting workaround);\n- the fetch side: both complete row closures re-fetched with a\n  100,000-row bound, the replayed root recompilation, and the\n  independent gate (source hash, site declarations, bindings, C1 marker\n  coverage) plus the per-fixture declaration-mutation refusal.\n\nOne durable store handle per batch invocation (cross-process reopen per\nbatch: full content-log validate-and-replay at open). The store is the\nshared accumulating measurement store; nothing else writes it.\n\n## 3. Ceilings (separate, finite, measured maxima + headroom)\n\nMeasured maxima are from the r8 run recorded in the outcomes file\n(maximum-based rule below).\n\n| Quantity | r8 measured maximum | Ceiling | Headroom / justification |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Storage, per fixture (store growth, content log) | 17,903,978 B | 24,000,000 B | +34 percent over the maximum; covers admission-scope/ordinal string growth and row-set jitter |\n| Storage, aggregate full-corpus store (155 fixtures, content log incl. lock) | 734,451,961 B | 1,000,000,000 B | +36 percent; a HEAD manifest and companion records are additional but small (declared before the run that adds them) |\n| Row-closure size, per tree (pre-stamp or stamped) | 12,193 rows | 20,000 rows | 1.64x; the fetch bound of 100,000 rows stays as the gate's own hard refusal limit |\n| Sites per fixture | 451 | 700 | +55 percent; corpus total 20,805 sites (equals the r2 independent inventory — informational cross-check) |\n| Elapsed, per fixture static gate | 136,513 ms (with per-fixture close/reopen protocol) | 300,000 ms | 2.2x; covers the slower early-protocol shape and store-replay growth |\n| Elapsed, per batch invocation (25 or fewer fixtures, one cross-process reopen) | about 12 min | 60 min | 5x; the operated hard background limit was 2 h per invocation |\n| Peak used heap | not durably recorded in r8 | NOT DECLARED | must be measured and declared by r9's own declaration before its run; cc3 v2.1 1 GiB figure is a baseline, not adopted |\n| Guest instructions / trace records | not applicable (static) | NOT DECLARED | r9 (C lanes) must declare; cc3 precedent 4,000,000 / 1,000,000 is a baseline only |\n\n## 4. Exact reproducible measurement procedure\n\nFrom the worktree root, per batch (the outcomes file is the progress\nrecord: recorded outcomes are final; an interrupted batch loses at most\nthe in-flight fixture, which re-runs under a fresh admission on resume):\n\n1. Write target/d1-corpus-run.edn with keys :D1_CORPUS_OUTCOMES\n   (collab/yang-clojure/1794422563000-engineer-d1-correspondence-r8.outcomes.edn),\n   :D1_CORPUS_STORE (target/d1-r8-corpus-store), :D1_CORPUS_BATCH 25 and\n   (first batch only) :D1_CORPUS_SPOT \"1\".\n2. Run clojure -M:test -n yang.latte-oracle-test -v\n   yang.latte-oracle-test/test-d1-corpus-static-gates — initializes the\n   outcomes file with all 155 ids on the first invocation, then records\n   the next 25-or-fewer not-yet-run fixtures atomically.\n3. Repeat step 2 until the summary reads fixture-count 155, pass 155,\n   refused 0, not-run 0.\n4. Without the run config present, the same deftest runs scratch mode\n   (3 fixtures, fresh scratch store, resumability + finality + spot-check\n   asserts) — the standing-battery form.\n\nActual r8 batch history (all against one shared store): batch 1 was\nkilled at a 10-minute background limit after 17 recorded fixtures, and\nits resume was stopped after 14 more (31 total) to remove the per-fixture\nclose/reopen (that reopen replays a growing content log: per-fixture wall\nhad reached 136 s); batches then ran with one store handle per invocation\n(cross-process reopen per batch) at 25, 25, 25, 25 and 24 fixtures — 155\ntotal. A killed batch's partial log tail is truncated at the next open\nby the store's own validate-then-truncate recovery; the re-run\nfixture's identical content dedupes, so per-fixture deltas remained\nadditive (sum of deltas = final store bytes, exactly).\n\n## 5. Pass/fail rule\n\nMaximum-based (cc3 v2 precedent): a ceiling is met only if the MAXIMUM\nobserved value is at or under it; any single sample over its ceiling is\nEXCEEDED. Medians and totals are informational. The r8 run's samples\nare recorded per fixture in the outcomes file and were not governed by\nthese ceilings (they precede this declaration).\n\n## 6. Limits of these measurements (named, not hidden)\n\nElapsed figures are warm-JVM wall clock on this host class and mix two\nreopen protocols (31 fixtures with per-fixture close/reopen, 124 with\nper-batch); storage figures are content-log bytes including dedup across\nfixtures (a per-fixture-fresh-store retention would be strictly larger);\nthe store has no HEAD manifest (a published retention artifact adds one\nplus ownership records); heap sampling existed only transiently per\nbatch and no durable heap maximum was recorded.\n# D1 correspondence: partial implementation findings\n\nStatus: **incomplete; not accepted and not verified**. No tagged verification run was performed, no companion record or verification result was published, and no relying consumer may treat these structural predicates as verification. No commit made.\n\n## Dispatch-time inventory refresh (partial)\n\n- Worktree `d1-correspondence` at `56cc5ca36855c0d047c11b7658e79cfc27d238e6`; tracked tree was clean at dispatch. The frozen `test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_site_tables.edn` SHA-256 is `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75` before and after this work. Its oracle identity is the EDN map with `:version 1` and per-fixture source hash and site table; this hash is a byte-archive check, not a Jing address.\n- Changed contracts proposed here: only a new, incomplete structural checker for the D1 and recipe closed key sets in `src/cljc/yang/correspondence.cljc`; no existing wire contract is changed. Structural acceptance is not address, schema-semantic, endpoint, coverage, derivation, or reconstruction validation. Its compilation-scoped uniqueness check is intentionally deferred because site integers restart per compilation. It does not provide the recipe profile, persisted inputs, discovery/retention schema, or result publication.\n- Oracle read-only dependencies: `kernel-compile`, `kernel-canonical-source-hash`, `kernel-stamp-sites`, `kernel-site-arguments`, `kernel-row`, `cc3-correspond`, and `cc3-oracle-binding` in `test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj`; `src/dev/latte_oracle_tables.clj` delegates to oracle private derivations and is insufficient as independent verification. The checked-in table is historical evidence; current compilation supplies an admission scope and ordinal at admission and retains `:admission-origin`, so the new run must persist those coordinates and its actual input closure, not mint old origins.\n- Occurrence and storage read-only dependencies: `yin.vm/occurrence-origin?`, `occurrences`, `application-occurrences`; `yin.vm.encoder/source-envelope`, `project`, `datom-entity-occurrences`; `dao.jing` canonical encoding, addressing, materialization and verified fetch. Published instruction provenance is outside the manifest in `yin.vm.linker.publish/published-instruction-provenance`. Semantic, stack and register images need their own execution contract and derivation checks; endpoint and multiplicity reconstruction still needs implementation.\n- cc3's test uses the post-port wrapper extraction `[:operands 1 :operands 1 :value]`; its stamped-to-pre-stamp traversal unwraps two applications. These must be independently reproduced and checked against `1794519755000-...reverification...findings.md` item 3, not assumed to be the pre-port three-application form.\n- The design's old `yin.repl.query/link-answer` location is stale: it is now private in `yin.shell.query`. The design's uncertainty about a frozen site-table schema and lack of a published provenance side artifact is historical: both exist at this base. `src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc` still exists. Port exception lowering is continuation-based; this work does not change it.\n- Datom-lane decision: retain cc3's **row-lane-only restriction** for D1. No entity-to-occurrence bridge is claimed. The exception-track ownership registry has not been conclusively established, and no files in the bridge surface were modified. Runtime migration or caller propagation is not claimed.\n- This inventory is not the complete §8 step 2 inventory: all reachable artifact families, their execution/derivation contracts, expansion ownership, discovery and retention owners, and exact registry status remain to be established before acceptance.\n\n## Tests and remaining work\n\nRed first: `clojure -M:test -n yang.correspondence-test` failed with missing `yang.correspondence`; after implementation it passed 1 test, 9 assertions. Direct clj-kondo 2026.08.04 on both new code/test files: 0 errors, 0 warnings. `bb test:sub:clj yang.correspondence` is not a registered subsystem. The full oracle suite was not run.\n\nThe closed structural predicates are merely a partial starting point. The remaining acceptance items include lossless Jing round-trip and retrieval, the exact recipe/profile and retained input closure, a real companion record, address and execution-contract checks, independent AST-derived declarations and bindings, C1/instruction coverage and multiplicity, scoped attribution over both C lanes, and a separate published verifier result. In particular, no independent verifier exists yet. The verifier must reject unknown evidence schema, failed reconstruction and missing retained admission without using `:trusted` as a substitute.\n\nNo D1 numeric preflight was declared or measured. cc3 v2.1's historical maxima (compiled-inclusive retained 8,924,640 B; aggregate serialized 1,169,745 B; peak used heap 272,026,880 B; pipeline 772 ms) are baselines only, not D1 measurements. A finite D1 declaration including trace capacity and bytes, workload, per-phase time, guest fuel and memory must precede any tagged run. No tagged run is authorized by this partial finding.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
